-- Agent.daml
-- ===========================================================================
-- DELEGATED / AGENT-INITIATED SETTLEMENT (the "agentic commerce, privately" angle).
-- ===========================================================================
-- A principal (a treasury desk, a fund, or an individual) grants an AUTHORISED
-- AGENT — a bot, an AI trading assistant, or a delegated manager — a MANDATE to
-- initiate DvP settlements ON THE PRINCIPAL'S BEHALF, but only within explicit
-- limits (instrument + per-trade amount cap). This is the same delegation shape
-- an institution uses to let a desk act for the firm, and it is enforced by the
-- LEDGER, not by a UI.
--
-- WHY THIS IS THE RIGHT DAML SHAPE
--   The agent must create a DvPProposal SIGNED BY THE PRINCIPAL (proposer =
--   principal) — because it commits the principal's asset. An agent cannot forge
--   the principal's signature. Daml delegation solves it: the principal signs the
--   mandate, so exercising a choice on the mandate brings the PRINCIPAL'S
--   AUTHORITY into scope. The agent (as controller) exercises that choice; inside
--   it, the principal's delegated authority creates the proposal. The mandate's
--   `maxAmount` is checked BEFORE the proposal is created — a ledger-enforced
--   limit, provable in Test:testAgentInitiatedDvP.
--
-- PRIVACY: the mandate is visible only to principal + agent. The wider network,
-- and even the eventual counterparty, never learn an agent (rather than the
-- principal) initiated the trade — the resulting proposal names only the
-- principal as proposer.
-- ===========================================================================

module Agent where

import Holding
import Settlement

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT A MANDATE HAS TO BOUND (and the half that was missing).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A delegation limit is only a limit if it bounds the VALUE the agent can move.
-- This mandate used to bound the SIZE alone: `assetAmount <= maxAmount`, with the
-- cash leg completely unconstrained. So an agent mandated to "sell up to 3 cETH"
-- could sell 3 cETH — the full mandated size, every other check satisfied — for ONE
-- USDC, and the ledger would sign it. The cap read like a risk control and was a
-- risk control on precisely nothing: a size limit with a free price is a licence to
-- give the position away, and the whole point of writing the delegation onto the
-- ledger is that the ledger, not a UI and not the agent's own good judgement, is
-- what enforces it. This is the shape of hole that matters most in an AGENTIC
-- setting, where the party exercising the choice is a bot that can be prompted,
-- mis-specified or compromised.
--
-- SO THE MANDATE NOW CARRIES A FLOOR ON THE UNIT PRICE, and both bounds are checked
-- together: at most `maxAmount` units, at no worse than `minUnitPrice` per unit.
-- `cashAmount >= assetAmount * minUnitPrice` is the natural statement of it — it
-- bounds the whole consideration rather than a per-unit ratio, so it cannot be
-- dodged by splitting a trade into pieces that each round in the agent's favour.
--
-- 🔴 THIS ADDS A FIELD TO `TradingMandate`. Anything constructing this record
-- positionally through Daml codegen must be updated. (At the time of writing no
-- hand-written Java in `backend/` or `backend-devnet/` constructs a TradingMandate —
-- only the generated model class exists — so the cost is a codegen regeneration.)
template TradingMandate
  with
    principal    : Party    -- the account owner delegating authority
    agent        : Party    -- the authorised agent (bot / AI / delegate)
    instrument   : Text     -- the ONLY asset the agent may sell, e.g. "cETH"
    maxAmount    : Decimal  -- per-settlement SIZE cap the agent may not exceed
    -- THE PRICE FLOOR — the worst unit price the principal will accept, quoted in
    -- the cash instrument of the trade. Together with `maxAmount` this bounds the
    -- VALUE the agent may move, which is the thing a delegation is actually for.
    minUnitPrice : Decimal
  where
    signatory principal   -- the principal signs: this IS the delegation of authority
    observer agent         -- the agent observes it (and controls the choice below)

    ensure maxAmount > 0.0 && minUnitPrice > 0.0

    -- NONCONSUMING so one mandate can drive many settlements. The agent initiates
    -- a DvPProposal on the principal's behalf, within the mandate limits.
    nonconsuming choice InitiateDvP : ContractId DvPProposal
      with
        counterparty    : Party
        auditor         : Party
        assetHoldingCid : ContractId Holding  -- the principal's asset holding to deliver
        cashHoldingCid  : ContractId Holding  -- the counterparty's cash holding
        assetAmount     : Decimal
        cashInstrument  : Text
        cashAmount      : Decimal
      controller agent
      do
        -- Validate the delegated asset holding against the mandate + the request.
        -- Fetch is authorised because the principal (owner/stakeholder of the
        -- asset) is in scope via the mandate signatory.
        asset <- fetch assetHoldingCid
        assertMsg "delegated holding must belong to the principal"
          (asset.owner == principal)
        assertMsg "agent may only trade the mandated instrument"
          (asset.instrumentId == instrument)
        assertMsg "stated asset amount must match the actual holding"
          (asset.amount == assetAmount)

        -- LIMITS ENFORCED BY THE LEDGER: an over-cap or under-priced request cannot
        -- create a proposal at all.
        --   SIZE  — at most `maxAmount` units per settlement.
        --   PRICE — at least `minUnitPrice` per unit, checked on the WHOLE
        --           consideration so it cannot be defeated by rounding a per-unit
        --           ratio. Without this second line the first one bounds nothing of
        --           economic consequence: an agent could deliver its full mandated
        --           size for a single unit of cash. See the header.
        assertMsg "settlement amount exceeds the mandate limit"
          (assetAmount <= maxAmount)
        assertMsg "settlement price is below the mandate's minimum unit price"
          (cashAmount >= assetAmount * minUnitPrice)
        -- A trade for nothing is not a trade. `DvPAgreement.Settle` computes
        -- `unitPrice = cashAmount / assetAmount`, so both legs must be positive for
        -- the receipt to mean anything.
        assertMsg "both legs of a delegated settlement must be positive"
          (assetAmount > 0.0 && cashAmount > 0.0)

        -- Create the proposal SIGNED BY THE PRINCIPAL (proposer = principal), using
        -- the principal's authority delegated through this mandate. From here the
        -- normal DvP flow applies: the counterparty Accepts, then Settle.
        create DvPProposal with
          proposer = principal
          counterparty
          auditor
          assetHoldingCid
          cashHoldingCid
          assetInstrument = instrument
          assetAmount
          cashInstrument
          cashAmount

    -- The principal can revoke the mandate at any time (consuming archive).
    choice Revoke : ()
      controller principal
      do pure ()
